# Exercise: Sleep and Measure
#
# Put `time.sleep-ms` and `time.nanos` together: pause for a
# given number of milliseconds, then return the actual elapsed
# duration in nanoseconds.
#
# This is the building block for "did that operation take longer
# than X?" checks — sleep stands in for whatever real work you'd
# normally measure.
#
# Your task: write `sleep-elapsed-nanos` — given a target sleep
# duration in milliseconds, sleep that long and return the
# observed elapsed time in nanoseconds.
#
# Stack effect: ( Int -- Int )
#
# Stack walk:
#
#   ( target-ms )
#   time.nanos                      → ( target-ms before )
#   swap                            → ( before target-ms )
#   time.sleep-ms                   → ( before )
#   time.nanos                      → ( before after )
#   swap i.-                        → ( after - before )
#
# Heads up: sleep is "at least N ms", so the elapsed time will be
# *at least* target-ms × 1,000,000 nanoseconds, often a little
# more. The test only asserts that elapsed > 0, which is true
# whenever the OS sleeps for any non-zero duration.
#
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: sleep-elapsed-nanos ( Int -- Int )
    # Your code here
    drop 0
;

: test-elapsed ( -- )
    5 sleep-elapsed-nanos 0 i.>

    # Do not edit below this line
    test.assert
;
